// Committed mesh-integrity baselines, read by `level_check`.
//
// Terrain is not perfectly watertight: ambiguous marching-cubes configurations
// in the cave noise leave a handful of open edges. These are pre-existing and
// not seam-related — stage 1 confirmed the count barely moves when the chunk
// size is quartered. The check is therefore relative: an error only when the
// count rises meaningfully above the figure recorded here.
//
// Re-commit these numbers when terrain generation or meshing changes on purpose.
// The count is per level *file*, not per segment definition: a segment
// instanced twice contributes twice. Re-commit when placement changes.
//
// Measured on branch level-segments-stage-1-5, and reproduced unchanged on
// level-segments-stage-2 — which is the regression test that the segment frame
// maths is identity-correct for a single segment at the origin.
//
// test_segments rose on level-segments-stage-3, from 310 to 1174, when the
// traversal primitives were added. That was measured rather than assumed: a
// primitive standing in free air contributes **zero** open edges, and the whole
// increase comes from the cells where a deck meets terrain. Unioning two
// independently-encoded density fields produces ambiguous marching-cubes
// configurations along the join, which is the same mechanism behind the cave
// figures above and not new here — but a route is *supposed* to meet the ground
// it crosses, so a level built from primitives meets it a great deal more
// often. Worth a mesh-quality pass; not a defect in the primitives.
(
    entries: [
        (level: "test_arena.level.ron", triangles: 191116, open_edges: 218),
        (level: "test_empty_terrain.level.ron", triangles: 98948, open_edges: 22),
        (level: "test_segments.level.ron", triangles: 223800, open_edges: 1174),
    ],
)
